{
  "id": "polars-int-arith",
  "engine": "polars",
  "engine_version": "py-2.1.0",
  "title": "Integer `+ - *`, unary `-` and `**`: wrap at the column's width",
  "verdict": "matches `overflow: SILENT` at every width",
  "area": "integers",
  "options": [
    "overflow"
  ],
  "constructs": [
    "+",
    "-",
    "*",
    "unary -",
    "**"
  ],
  "settings": {},
  "description": [
    "Integer arithmetic wraps at the operands' width (two's complement, Rust's `wrapping_add`, `wrapping_sub`, `wrapping_mul`, `wrapping_neg`): `Int8` 127 + 1 is -128, `-MIN` is `MIN`. No setting makes it raise or saturate. Polars keeps the width of the operands; a literal takes the column's type.",
    "`**` on integers wraps too (`2 ** 63` is `-2⁶³` in `Int64`); a negative integer exponent raises (`InvalidOperationError`, the exponent is cast to `u32`)."
  ],
  "reference": "`add`, `subtract`, `multiply`, `negate` with `overflow: ERROR` when the plan omits it (#202); `SILENT` when the plan asks. Polars gives `SILENT` natively; `ERROR` needs a guard, at each width.",
  "status": "open",
